<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as German Democratic Republic, GDR</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the German Democratic Republic, was the communist state formed in October 1949 from the Soviet occupation zone of Germany. Ruled by the Socialist Unity Party, it abolished the historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-of-germany">Länder</a> in 1952 and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> in 1961. After the peaceful revolution of 1989 its restored states joined the Federal Republic in October 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-germany.jpg" alt="East Germany" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Deutsche Demokratische Republik Flaggenentwurf: Fritz Behrendt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_German_Democratic_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 23 May 1949 the western sectors, controlled by France, the United Kingdom and the United States, were merged into the Federal Republic of Germany; on 7 October 1949 the Soviet Zone became the <b>German Democratic Republic</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Some one-party states permit minor parties that accept the ruling party’s leadership, as China does with eight small parties and as <b>East Germany</b> did with its allied bloc parties; others allow independent candidates, chosen with care.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>Governments modelled on the Soviet Union took power with Soviet help in Bulgaria, Czechoslovakia, <b>East Germany</b>, Poland, Hungary and Romania after the Second World War; Tito’s Yugoslavia broke with Stalin in 1948, and the Cuban revolutionaries who overthrew Batista in 1959 became Marxist–Leninist.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Auferstanden aus Ruinen&quot; (&quot;Risen from Ruins&quot;) was the national anthem of <b>East Germany</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auferstanden-aus-ruinen">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</a></li><li><blockquote><b>East Germany</b> had its own anthem, &quot;Auferstanden aus Ruinen&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;Lied der Partei&quot; (Song of the Party), also known as &quot;Die Partei hat immer recht&quot; (The Party Is Always Right), was the anthem of the Socialist Unity Party of Germany (SED), the ruling party of <b>East Germany</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lied-der-partei">Lied der Partei</a></li></ul></section>
